{
  "dom_id": "step-q214597",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Express the intersection of intervals $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)$ as an inequality.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "We start by sketching the two intervals $(-1,2)$ and $[0,3)$ separately:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/a5a6b966c5d98863cfed20ccb13f3a1ca5413887f228f091d504bb7cd0001b37.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/823bf1d62aadc91ac15e52260a9919622358144865f0522f34389d48f8c11273.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191341/assets/9c7ea77f9699601b97ff38a886af6418b44fa9568009aaa4b36be17efa03cbee.png)\n\nThus, $(-1,2)\\cap [0,3)=[0,2).$ This corresponds to the inequality $0\\le x<2.$",
